\begin_layout Title
Analysis of a series of experiments of particle tracking through heterogeneous media with increaing semivariogram range
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Prof.
 dr.ir.
 T.N.Olsthoorn
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
2024-03-25
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Intro
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A series of experiments has been conducted with particle tracking through a heterogeneous cross section with increasing semi-variogram length in the direction of flow.
 The Modflow model was 50 layers by 2001 cells of 1 by 1 m,
 allowing to in each experiment track 1000 particles over a distance of exactly 2000 m and register their breakthrough times at 100 m intervals up to 2000 m and also to allow locating particles at a series of observation times.
 The conductivity field was generated by gstools using the exponential model with a varying horizontal integration length 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{h}$
\end_inset

 and the a vertical integration length 
\begin_inset Formula $Iv$
\end_inset

 that was kept constant at 1 m in all experiments.
 The exponential field is defined by the covariance functions in the horizontal and vertical direction in which 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x$
\end_inset

 is the horizontal lag and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta z$
\end_inset

 the verticl lag (distance between points)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C=C_{0}\left(1-e^{-\frac{\Delta x}{I_{v}}}\right),\,\,\,C=C_{0}\left(1-e^{-\frac{\Delta z}{I_{h}}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The horizontal integration length in the experiments was 1,
 2,
 4,
 8,
 16,
 32,
 64,
 and 128 m.
 The conductivity field is lognormal with a geometric mean 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{G}=0.89$
\end_inset

 m/d and a log varance 
\begin_inset Formula $var\left(\ln k\right)=6.6$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\left(\ln k\right)=2.6$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\exp\left(\sigma\left(\ln k\right)\right)=13.4$
\end_inset

) Hence the bandwith of the conductivity as a factor is 
\begin_inset Formula $1/180<1/13.4<1<13.4<180$
\end_inset

 if the bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $=-2\sigma<-\sigma<0<\sigma<2\sigma$
\end_inset

 are considered on the 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln k$
\end_inset

 scale.
 Therefore,
 the bandwidth is quite large as is the range of of the integration length (semi-variogram range) considered in the experiments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiments yield breakthrough curves at distances that were chosen from 100 m to 2000 m at 100 m intervals.
 A nognormal cdf was fitted to each of the and the parameters noted for further analysis.
 Next to this,
 cumulative particle location curves were obtained for the tracking results for different points in time.
 Also through these curves cdfs for the lognormal distribution were fitted and the paramters noted.
 For longer times,
 the later location curves tend to increasingly loose points that were already captured at the end of the model,
 at 2000 m.
 The fits for these cuvers therefore,
 fail and must be omitted form further analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left(t-loc\right)\sim N\left(\mu,\sigma\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\lang american
Analysis of the lognormal distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathtt{if}\,T\sim LogNormal\left(\mu,\sigma\right)\rightarrow\log T\sim N\left(\mu,\sigma\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
When retrieving the lognormal distribution or generating it,
 it has three parameters scale,
 loc,
 shape,
 where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\ln\left(scale\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=shape$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula $loc$
\end_inset

=linear shift in the time domain (real world domain)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t\sim LogNormal\left(\mu,\sigma,\mathtt{loc}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
is equivalent to saying
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left(t-\mathtt{loc}\right)\sim N\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Or,
 rearranged
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t=\mathtt{loc}+\exp\left(X\right),\,\,\,\,X\sim N\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
Hence,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathtt{loc}$
\end_inset

 acts as a shift in the time domain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
It's the minimum value the variable 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 can take – the distribution is defined for 
\begin_inset Formula $t>\mathtt{loc}.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
It's often used to delay the onset of the lognormal process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Further properties of the lognormal distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathtt{mean}\left(t\right)=\mathtt{loc}+\exp\left(\mu+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathtt{median}\left(t\right)=\mathtt{loc}+\exp\left(\mu\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathtt{mode}\left(t\right)=\mathtt{loc}+\exp\left(\mu-\sigma^{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Head and tail quantiles may be useful,
 but more so in log-space than in real-space due to the skewness of the lognormal distribution.
 The quantile times s 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{05}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{16}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{50}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{84}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{95}$
\end_inset

 can also easily obtained from a given cdf or pdf distribution,
 but these too are less informative for the lognormal distribution than for the normal distribution.
 
\end_layout

