\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The particle tracking experiments were done using a vertical cross section of 50x2001 cells of 1x1 m2 with a given gradient created by using fixed heads at both ends.
 The conductivity field was generated by gstools as an exponential model as outlined above with 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{h}$
\end_inset

 different for each cross section and 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{v}$
\end_inset

 kept at 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

m for all cross sections.
 The parameters used are in the heading of the different pictures with the results.
 The horizontal integration length 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{h}$
\end_inset

 was for each subsequent cross section increased from 1,
 2,
 4,
 8,
 16,
 32,
 64 adn 128 m to provide a wide range of values for the horizontal connectivity between model cells.
 The conductivity values used for generating the condcutivity fields were lognormally distributed with a geometric mean 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{G}=0.89$
\end_inset

 m/d and a log variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $var\left(\ln k\right)=6.6$
\end_inset

.
 Porositie was 31%.
 The gradient used in each experiment is also mentioned in the figures with the results.
 The original gradient of 
\begin_inset Formula $J=-0.0036$
\end_inset

 yielded a total discharge through the model of 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=1.22$
\end_inset

 m
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

/d.
 The gradients for subsequent models were adapted such that the total model discharge was the same in all the experiments.
 The actual gradient used is also in the header of the figures.
 It's because of the same total discharge that the breakthrough curves for all experiments lie similary on the time axis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The breakthrough times of the particles in each experiment have been sorted and scaled to obtain normalized breakthrough curves that thus are numerically derived cumulative density functions.
 Next,
 lognormal cdfs were fitted using scipy.stats.lognormal.fit(data).
 This also yielded the parameters of the fitted cdfs.
 The numerically derived data are plotted together with their fitted cdf's in the figure,
 while the paramters of the fitted cdfs are shown in the legend for each graph.
 With these parameters the pdf for the graphs can also be plotted which is done in the bottom graphs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Both cdfs and pdfs show points that mark the mode,
 medium and mean time.
 Note that for the lognormal distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{mode}<t_{med}<t_{mean}$
\end_inset

.
 Extra points indicated with circles are relative to are at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=t_{0}+\alpha\left(t_{mode}-t_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=0.2,0.5,1.0,2.0,5.0$
\end_inset

 respectively.
 Hence the third circle indicates 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{mode}$
\end_inset

.
 For these skewed graphs,
 these times relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{mode}$
\end_inset

 were found more insightful than the ususal points that mark fractions of the cdf (e.g.
 the times when 5%,
 16%,
 50% 845 and 95% of the cdf was reached).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
We will analyze the graphs based on the fitted cdfs and pdfs.
 The lognormal distribution expressed in its parameters is defined by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\left(t;\mu,\sigma,t_{0}\right)=\frac{1}{\left(t-t_{0}\right)\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{\left(\ln\left(t-t_{0}\right)-\mu\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right),\,\,\,\,t>t_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

