\begin_layout Section

\lang american
Lognormal distribution inverted
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The obtained time-breakthrough curves allowed a good fit by a lognormal distribution.
 This implies that we may analyze the results based on the parameters of the distribution that results from the fit.
 The time the particles took to reach a given distance from the source can be inverted into an average velocity of each particle conditioned for this distance.
 These velocities too are a distribution.
 It is clear that when a distribution for the time breakthrough curves is given mathematically it should be convertible to that for the particle velocities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Let the lognormal distribution for the breakthrough times be given as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
T & \sim LogNormal\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)\\
\ln T & \sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{X}\left(x\right)=\frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{\left(x-\mu\right)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we want the pdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $T=e^{x}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\ln T$
\end_inset

.
 Use the rule for the change of variables:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{T}\left(t\right)=f_{X}\left(\ln t\right).\left|\frac{d}{dt}\ln t\right|=f_{X}\left(x\right).\frac{1}{t}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
This yields the lognormal distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 still for the underlying normal distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{T}\left(t\right)=\frac{1}{t\sigma_{T}\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{\left(\ln t-\mu_{T}\right)}{2\sigma_{T}^{2}}\right),\,\,\,\,t>0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Now step over to the distribution of the average velocities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{V}\left(v\right)=f_{T}\left(\frac{L}{v}\right).\left|\frac{d}{dv}\left(\frac{L}{v}\right)\right|=f_{T}\left(\frac{L}{v}\right).\frac{L}{v^{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Plug this into the previous lognormal distribution to get
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{V}\left(v\right)=\frac{1}{v\sigma_{T}\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{\left(\ln\left(L/v\right)-\mu_{T}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma_{T}^{2}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mu_{V} & =\ln L-\mu_{T}\\
\sigma_{V} & =\sigma_{T}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{V}\left(v\right)=\frac{1}{v\sigma_{V}\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{\left(\ln v-\mu_{V}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma_{V}^{2}}\right),\,\,\,\,v>0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{N}\left(\mu_{V},\sigma_{V}^{2}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\ln L-\mu_{T},\sigma_{T}^{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

